WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:03.167
-Hey, guys, we're here
at McNally Jackson bookstore

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:04.567
in midtown Manhattan, and we're
about to surprise some people

00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:06.567
who think that they're part
of a promotional campaign

00:00:06.567 --> 00:00:09.501
for Michelle Obama's new book,
"The Light We Carry."

00:00:09.501 --> 00:00:11.868
They're going to press a button
to hear inspirational quotes

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:14.100
from the former First Lady,
Michelle Obama.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.901
But what they don't know is
that the real Michelle Obama

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:18.734
will be reading them
the quotes live.

00:00:18.734 --> 00:00:20.467
-That is me.
-Ready?

00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:21.701
-Let's do this.

00:00:21.701 --> 00:00:27.434
♪♪

00:00:27.434 --> 00:00:30.133
-I was going to
get this one, too.

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:32.200
-Please press the button.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.901
♪♪

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:37.701
Please press the button to hear
an inspirational quote

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:40.000
from Michelle O.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.267
-We practice our faith
in the smallest of ways.

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:48.667
With it, we are saying, "I can."

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.968
We are saying "I care."

00:00:52.968 --> 00:00:56.334
-Simon says, touch your head.

00:00:57.501 --> 00:01:02.400
Simon says,
touch your shoulders.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.400
Press the button.

00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:08.367
Simon didn't say.

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.734
Now pose for a picture.

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:14.734
Three, two, one.

00:01:14.734 --> 00:01:16.467
Turn around.
-Smile, ladies.

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:18.334
-[ Gasps ]
-Turn around, please.

00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:19.901
-Simon says.
-Oh, my gosh!

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:23.501
-Smile.
-So nice to meet you.

00:01:23.501 --> 00:01:26.601
-Oh, my gosh!
-Hi. I'm Jimmy.

00:01:26.601 --> 00:01:27.834
-Press the button.

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:33.667
-We practice our faith
in the smallest of ways.

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:36.067
With it, we are saying, "I can."

00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:38.834
We are saying, "I care."

00:01:38.834 --> 00:01:42.601
-Simon says touch your head.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:46.901
Simon says scream.

00:01:46.901 --> 00:01:49.133
-[ Screams ]

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:50.601
-Louder.

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:52.567
-[ Screams ]

00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:54.634
-Simon didn't say
that last one.

00:01:54.634 --> 00:01:56.901
-But Michelle Obama did.
-You lose.

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:04.133
But maybe you didn't lose.
-Oh, my God!

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:08.133
Oh, my God!

00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:09.200
Wow!

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.968
-Because my mother
had faith in me,

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:14.901
I had faith in me, too.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.667
-Mother's Day is coming up.

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:19.334
What are you getting
your mother?

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:20.400
-Let's see.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.100
-You can pick up
a few books right now.

00:02:27.934 --> 00:02:30.434
-Please buy my book.

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:33.167
Pretty please?

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:34.734
-"Pretty please
with sugar on top."

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:36.100
-With sugar on top?

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.434
-Please grab a book and look.
-Are you ready?

00:02:38.434 --> 00:02:41.234
-Oh, my God!
-It's Michelle Obama.

00:02:41.234 --> 00:02:42.734
-Buy my book.

00:02:42.734 --> 00:02:46.868
Tools evolve over time.

00:02:46.868 --> 00:02:50.400
And speaking of time,
it's time to dance.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.534
-Oh.
-Show us your best moves.

00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:00.267
Go.

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:01.267
Drop it low.

00:03:02.734 --> 00:03:04.167
To the floor.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.534
Down lower.

00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:11.601
Go, go, go, go.

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:16.033
-Now pose for a picture.
Three, two...

00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:18.968
-Go.
-[ Screams ] Oh, my God!

00:03:18.968 --> 00:03:19.968
Oh, my God!

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.834
-You've got some great moves.
-Oh, my God!

00:03:26.234 --> 00:03:31.968
-Thank you just for going
through this entire journey

00:03:31.968 --> 00:03:33.534
with grace.

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:34.968
That's what you are.

00:03:34.968 --> 00:03:38.834
And to have the opportunity
to say that to you, thank you.

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:41.601
-Oh, sweetie,
that means everything.

00:03:41.601 --> 00:03:43.367
It means everything to me.

00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:46.033
-Ahhh!
-Ahhh!

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:48.133
[ Camera shutter clicks ]

00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:49.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:51.334
-And thank you for stopping by.
And thank you for --

00:03:51.334 --> 00:03:52.667
-Thanks for having me.

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:54.834
-Thanks for playing yet again.
We did another fun --

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:56.367
-We have played so much.

00:03:56.367 --> 00:03:57.901
-We've had so many fun bits.

00:03:57.901 --> 00:04:00.367
-You know, the first
crazy skit I did,

00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:02.634
if you all recall, was --

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:04.200
-In the White --
-In the White House.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.467
And we did a potato-sack race
in the East Room.

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:08.334
[ Laughter ]
-That's right. We did.

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:11.200
-You were the first silly I had.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.501
-I like being the first silly.
I love that.

00:04:12.501 --> 00:04:15.734
-So, thank you for that.
-I gotta say, I love having you.

00:04:15.734 --> 00:04:17.067
I love talking to you,
obviously.

00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:21.367
But to see people's reaction
to you next to you,

00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:23.734
it makes me feel so good.
People love you.

00:04:23.734 --> 00:04:26.100
I mean, we all love you,
but we really --

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.968
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Aww, you guys...

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:30.434
-You gotta...

00:04:30.434 --> 00:04:32.968
-Look, I don't
take it for granted.

00:04:32.968 --> 00:04:35.701
Look, we don't get out much.
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:35.701 --> 00:04:40.000
-So it's just nice to hear
what it all meant to people,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.267
particularly young people.

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:43.801
So I don't take
any of that for granted.

00:04:43.801 --> 00:04:46.701
-Do you ever get starstruck
anymore, or...?

00:04:46.701 --> 00:04:49.434
-Oh. Pfft!
Questlove.

00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:51.334
-Oh, come on.
Oh, come on.

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:55.000
-Hi, hi!
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.901
Absolutely I get starstruck.
-You do?

00:04:56.901 --> 00:05:00.167
-Well, maybe not exactly
right now, in this moment,

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:02.634
but in the course of my life...
-What?

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:05.200
-I mean, you just imagine
the people that we met.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.067
Stevie? You know,
when I first met Stevie Wonder?

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:09.634
-Yes, of course.
-I mean, I was just like,

00:05:09.634 --> 00:05:12.334
"Stevie!"
I fangirled out.

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:15.000
-You have to, yeah.
-You know, Prince, I mean...

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:16.567 --> 00:05:19.167
Prince played
at the White House

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:21.501
months before he passed,
actually.

00:05:21.501 --> 00:05:24.234
And just -- I know.
It was just --

00:05:24.234 --> 00:05:26.467
-Isn't he great,
the way that Prince was,

00:05:26.467 --> 00:05:28.501
also, as a person?

00:05:28.501 --> 00:05:31.167
I've had conversations with
your husband about this, too,

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:33.400
that everyone is kind of
who they should be.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.067
-Yeah.
-Prince is a magical --

00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:38.634
-He appears.
-Yes!

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:40.601
Yes, he kind of --
Like, a puff of smoke.

00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:42.000
He just kind of shows up.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.567
-He's in the Blue Room,
standing in front

00:05:43.567 --> 00:05:46.167
of President Obama,
and he's just like, "Yo."

00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:47.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:47.601 --> 00:05:49.300
-He's just cool.
-Still right here.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.834
-Yeah, exactly.
-So, yes,

00:05:50.834 --> 00:05:52.167
I fangirl out all the time.

00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:54.601
-Congratulations on
"The Light We Carry."

00:05:54.601 --> 00:05:59.934
It's another New York Times
Best Seller.

00:05:59.934 --> 00:06:03.167
And the book -- You dedicate
the book to your parents.

00:06:03.167 --> 00:06:06.100
-Oh, my God.
-Which I do love.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:07.801
You've talked
about the influence

00:06:07.801 --> 00:06:09.767
that your father's had on you,

00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:13.000
but, also, there's a whole
chapter here about your mom.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.467
-Marian.
-Yes.

00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:16.100
-She deserved a chapter.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.300
She's -- She's --
You know, my parents

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.801
are where I get
any wisdom that I have.

00:06:20.801 --> 00:06:25.167
And the fact that she allowed me
to even say her name in the book

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:27.901
is a big deal,
because Marian Robinson

00:06:27.901 --> 00:06:30.567
isn't impressed with anything
at any time.

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:32.400
So I had to ask permission.
I was like, "Mom --"

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.734
We were trying
to get her to write a book.

00:06:34.734 --> 00:06:36.634
She's like, "Nobody wants
to hear what I have to say."

00:06:36.634 --> 00:06:37.934
I was like,
"No, mom, everybody..."

00:06:37.934 --> 00:06:39.767
-Everybody wants to hear, yeah.
-"...wants to hear

00:06:39.767 --> 00:06:42.033
what you have to say."
-She does have some great

00:06:42.033 --> 00:06:44.501
tried-and-true parenting
in the book.

00:06:44.501 --> 00:06:48.567
Number five is "Come Home.
We will always like you here."

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:50.501
-Yeah. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:50.501 --> 00:06:52.534
That is true.
-I mean, let me tell you

00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:54.200
how grounding that is.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.467
And for parents,
it's important to remember.

00:06:56.467 --> 00:06:59.868
Because she said that
because she knew

00:06:59.868 --> 00:07:02.467
that, as kids of color,
as women,

00:07:02.467 --> 00:07:05.534
as a woman, young woman,
young Black woman in the world,

00:07:05.534 --> 00:07:07.534
that I would be confronted
with a lot of people

00:07:07.534 --> 00:07:11.234
who would set my bar low for me.

00:07:11.234 --> 00:07:16.601
You know, she knew we were
going to hear racial insults.

00:07:16.601 --> 00:07:19.000
She knew what we were
going to confront.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.167
And she never wanted us
to get our self-worth

00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:25.501
from outside the home
because we couldn't count on it.

00:07:25.501 --> 00:07:29.567
So that maxim is
"Don't sweat the small stuff."

00:07:29.567 --> 00:07:32.734
Don't lose it because
a teacher doesn't like you,

00:07:32.734 --> 00:07:35.133
because somebody
called you a bad word.

00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:38.334
Don't let your self-esteem
be based on that.

00:07:38.334 --> 00:07:40.767
You know, get your
self-worth at home.

00:07:40.767 --> 00:07:44.934
And so that's a powerful thing
for all parents to remember.

00:07:44.934 --> 00:07:47.667
But it's an important thing
for adults to remember

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:51.534
as we deal with young people,
that our words to them matter.

00:07:51.534 --> 00:07:52.667
You know?
-Yeah.

00:07:52.667 --> 00:07:54.868
-What we say,
how we treat them,

00:07:54.868 --> 00:07:58.834
it has an impact, because
some kids don't have a home

00:07:58.834 --> 00:08:01.300
to go to
to feel that love.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.501
So I think about that every time
I interact with a kid,

00:08:04.501 --> 00:08:05.834
that my interaction with them

00:08:05.834 --> 00:08:09.300
may be the time they feel safe
and they feel at home.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.234
So I want everybody
to think about that.

00:08:12.234 --> 00:08:13.901
-That goes so far.
-So I got that at home.

00:08:13.901 --> 00:08:16.300
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I love that.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.734
How, uh...

00:08:18.734 --> 00:08:20.601
How are the kids?

00:08:20.601 --> 00:08:22.667
-Kids are good. Those two.
-Sasha and Malia.

00:08:22.667 --> 00:08:24.133
-They're not kids.
-Are they still --

00:08:24.133 --> 00:08:25.601
-They are grown --
-They're 7 years old?

00:08:25.601 --> 00:08:27.467
How old are they?
[ Laughter ]

00:08:27.467 --> 00:08:28.567
-That's how the world
sees them.

00:08:28.567 --> 00:08:31.234
Twent--
They'll be 25 and 22.

00:08:31.234 --> 00:08:33.968
-When did that happen?
-Yeah. You all are old.

00:08:33.968 --> 00:08:35.901
[ Laughter ]
When did that happen?

00:08:35.901 --> 00:08:38.501
-I know.
Before our very eyes.

00:08:38.501 --> 00:08:42.334
All I can say is,
no more babies, no more tuition.

00:08:42.334 --> 00:08:44.167
[ Laughter ]
Bye.

00:08:44.167 --> 00:08:46.701
It's like, "Here's your life."
-"There you go."

00:08:46.701 --> 00:08:47.934
-"Good luck."
-Yeah, "Let me know

00:08:47.934 --> 00:08:49.067
how it works out."
Yeah.

00:08:49.067 --> 00:08:50.934
But now they're
living together, right?

00:08:50.934 --> 00:08:53.400
They're roommates?
-Right now they are, yep, yeah.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.801
-Do you visit them?
-We visited them once.

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:59.667
-Okay.
-When we're your parents,

00:08:59.667 --> 00:09:01.968
you don't really want us
around that much, you know?

00:09:01.968 --> 00:09:03.434
[ Laughter ]
-Really?

00:09:03.434 --> 00:09:06.234
-So -- But we did get
invited over for cocktails

00:09:06.234 --> 00:09:08.501
before dinner because we had
to take them out to dinner,

00:09:08.501 --> 00:09:10.033
and they invited us
over for cocktails.

00:09:10.033 --> 00:09:11.200
-No, no, no.
-Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.367
-No, you don't drink a cocktail.
-Oh. Pfft!

00:09:14.367 --> 00:09:16.534
Not only did they drink it,
but they made one.

00:09:16.534 --> 00:09:19.334
-No!
-That was a bad martini.

00:09:19.334 --> 00:09:20.634
[ Laughter ]

00:09:20.634 --> 00:09:23.000
It was very weak,
in a tumbling glass.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.534
[ Laughter ]

00:09:25.534 --> 00:09:26.901
You know,
they stumbled through --

00:09:26.901 --> 00:09:29.801
It was mostly vermouth and ice.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:29.801 --> 00:09:31.067
-I still do the same thing.

00:09:31.067 --> 00:09:32.501
-Well, maybe it
wasn't even vermouth,

00:09:32.501 --> 00:09:34.534
because I don't even think
that they knew vermouth

00:09:34.534 --> 00:09:35.901
was in a martini.
-Yeah, that's exactly it.

00:09:35.901 --> 00:09:37.300
But they're trying to
impress you with a martini.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:38.834
-Oh, yeah.
Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:09:38.834 --> 00:09:41.434
And a charcuterie tray.
-Ooh!

00:09:41.434 --> 00:09:43.901
-Which they complained
about the price of cheese.

00:09:43.901 --> 00:09:45.133
[ Laughter ]

00:09:45.133 --> 00:09:46.400
-That's good.
-Malia's like,

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.133
"Do you know how much I paid
for that cheese?

00:09:48.133 --> 00:09:49.701
[ Laughter ]
And I was like, "Yeah, girl,

00:09:49.701 --> 00:09:52.067
you eat my cheese all the time."
-Exactly.

00:09:52.067 --> 00:09:54.033
-Now you --
-Never worried about the price

00:09:54.033 --> 00:09:56.033
when you eat
my charcuterie tray.

00:09:56.033 --> 00:09:57.968
It's like...
-You've always said

00:09:57.968 --> 00:09:59.601
when we've talked about the kids

00:09:59.601 --> 00:10:01.300
that you wanted to give them
the most normal childhood

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:03.033
that you possibly can
while you're living

00:10:03.033 --> 00:10:05.434
in the White House,
which has got to be tough.

00:10:05.434 --> 00:10:08.767
But I remember when you were
saying even Barack

00:10:08.767 --> 00:10:10.667
coached their basketball team?
-Yes.

00:10:10.667 --> 00:10:13.100
Yeah, he was -- he was dad
in the White House.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:14.667
-How did you sneak out
and do that, though?

00:10:14.667 --> 00:10:17.567
How is that possible?
-You know, it's amazing.

00:10:17.567 --> 00:10:20.000
You know, and he wasn't assigned
to be the coach.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.467
This was fourth-grade rec league
for little girls.

00:10:23.467 --> 00:10:26.367
Right? Sasha was playing,
and he and Reggie Love,

00:10:26.367 --> 00:10:27.467
who was his aide at the time,

00:10:27.467 --> 00:10:29.334
who was a Division I player
for Duke,

00:10:29.334 --> 00:10:32.901
they would sit and watch these
girls not have their shoes tied,

00:10:32.901 --> 00:10:34.501
they weren't running plays,

00:10:34.501 --> 00:10:36.901
because it was a parent-coached
league, right?

00:10:36.901 --> 00:10:38.467
-Yeah.
Also, they're in fourth grade.

00:10:38.467 --> 00:10:39.734
-They're in fourth grade,

00:10:39.734 --> 00:10:42.367
but Barack Obama felt
that they could do better.

00:10:42.367 --> 00:10:44.267
[ Laughter ]

00:10:44.267 --> 00:10:46.934
So each game,
he would slide closer down

00:10:46.934 --> 00:10:48.567
to the players' bench
and whisper to

00:10:48.567 --> 00:10:50.501
the parent coaches,
who I'm sure were like,

00:10:50.501 --> 00:10:54.434
"Oh, my God, it's the President
telling me to run a play."

00:10:54.434 --> 00:10:55.834
Eventually, he took over.

00:10:55.834 --> 00:11:00.067
He had them practicing,
he taught them plays.

00:11:00.067 --> 00:11:02.000
And the funny thing is, like,

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.400
it wasn't only Sasha
on the team,

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.667
but it was Maisy Biden,
Joe's youngest granddaughter.

00:11:06.667 --> 00:11:07.767
They're very good friends.

00:11:07.767 --> 00:11:10.067
So imagine it,
on any given Sunday,

00:11:10.067 --> 00:11:13.000
in a rec gym in Chevy Chase --

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:15.467
'Cause we all came out, right?
-Yeah.

00:11:15.467 --> 00:11:17.634
-You're playing
against the Vipers.

00:11:17.634 --> 00:11:19.734
That was their name.
They were the Vipers.

00:11:19.734 --> 00:11:21.434
-Yeah.
-It would be the President,

00:11:21.434 --> 00:11:24.801
the Vice President,
the first, second lady,

00:11:24.801 --> 00:11:28.100
all the kids,
and all of our details.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:29.934
[ Laughter ]

00:11:29.934 --> 00:11:31.801
So you imagine
being the poor kids

00:11:31.801 --> 00:11:33.767
on the other team...
[ Laughter ]

00:11:33.767 --> 00:11:35.801
...with Joe yelling,
"Maisy, pass the ball!"

00:11:35.801 --> 00:11:37.534
[ Laughter ]
"Box out!"

00:11:37.534 --> 00:11:39.934
You know, at the time, I would
have to tell them, "Chill out."

00:11:39.934 --> 00:11:41.901
"You know, this is --
these kids --

00:11:41.901 --> 00:11:43.400
I think that little girl
is crying

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:44.868
'cause the President's
yelling at them."

00:11:44.868 --> 00:11:46.033
[ Laughter ]
-How did they end up doing?

00:11:46.033 --> 00:11:47.400
-They won the championship.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:49.100
-Come on!
That's exactly right!

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:50.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:50.701 --> 00:11:52.067
-♪ The Vipers ♪

00:11:52.067 --> 00:11:54.734
-Guys, more with Michelle Obama
when we come back.

00:11:54.734 --> 00:11:57.033
I noticed that you went back --

00:11:57.033 --> 00:12:00.200
you went back
to the White House recently.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:03.133
You haven't been back.
-No. Wasn't invited.

00:12:03.133 --> 00:12:05.267
[ Laughter ]

00:12:05.267 --> 00:12:06.934
Ooh, shade.

00:12:06.934 --> 00:12:09.300
[ Laughter ]

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:11.968
-Wow. Wow.

00:12:11.968 --> 00:12:15.701
I'm adding a chapter right here.
Hold on.

00:12:15.701 --> 00:12:17.834
-Oh, yeah.
-And so you return.

00:12:17.834 --> 00:12:19.934
What did it feel like
to go back

00:12:19.934 --> 00:12:23.167
and you go, "Oh, yeah, I lived
a lot of time in this place"?

00:12:23.167 --> 00:12:25.801
-We were -- We were good
when it was time to go.

00:12:25.801 --> 00:12:28.934
No, it was really
a beautiful experience,

00:12:28.934 --> 00:12:30.868
because, you know,
that's a tradition.

00:12:30.868 --> 00:12:33.133
You do your official portraits.

00:12:33.133 --> 00:12:37.033
The next president is supposed
to invite you back to hang them.

00:12:37.033 --> 00:12:38.567
We were never invited back.

00:12:38.567 --> 00:12:41.901
So these pictures had been done
for a long, long time.

00:12:41.901 --> 00:12:45.167
But it's also a chance
for the staff

00:12:45.167 --> 00:12:47.834
to come back and reminisce
and to be together.

00:12:47.834 --> 00:12:51.467
It's a -- It's a ritual.
So it was good to see everybody.

00:12:51.467 --> 00:12:53.167
It was good to see
all of the staff.

00:12:53.167 --> 00:12:55.868
I mean, as quiet as it's kept,

00:12:55.868 --> 00:12:58.033
my girls lived
in the White House

00:12:58.033 --> 00:13:00.367
longer they lived any house.
-Yeah.

00:13:00.367 --> 00:13:01.834
-So that's where they grew up.

00:13:01.834 --> 00:13:05.901
So to go back and to see all
the staff that are still there,

00:13:05.901 --> 00:13:08.434
to see all the people
that they grew up with,

00:13:08.434 --> 00:13:09.734
it was really --

00:13:09.734 --> 00:13:11.367
Well, the girls weren't there
at the time,

00:13:11.367 --> 00:13:13.667
but it was good for
Barack and I to see everyone.

00:13:13.667 --> 00:13:15.434
-Do you ever have dreams
that you still live there?

00:13:15.434 --> 00:13:18.767
-Oh! No. Oh, I'm sorry.

00:13:18.767 --> 00:13:20.934
I didn't mean to say that
like that.

00:13:20.934 --> 00:13:24.400
It's like -- No, no, I don't --
I don't have dreams.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:27.067
-You don't ever go like,
"Oh, yeah, I'll go down

00:13:27.067 --> 00:13:30.767
the steps here and do that."
-No. No. Funny story.

00:13:30.767 --> 00:13:33.200
At Biden's inauguration,

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:36.000
all the formers gathered,

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:38.300
and they were doing
a public service announcement.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:40.033
And then we had to leave.

00:13:40.033 --> 00:13:43.434
And this is the first time
Barack attended --

00:13:43.434 --> 00:13:45.467
I think he was there
when somebody else's

00:13:45.467 --> 00:13:47.801
presidential motorcade was out.

00:13:47.801 --> 00:13:49.934
So he walks out,
and usually the first car up

00:13:49.934 --> 00:13:51.434
is the President, right?

00:13:51.434 --> 00:13:55.601
And he goes walking to the first
car, and his security is like,

00:13:55.601 --> 00:13:59.534
"Sir, um, that's not --
that's not your car."

00:13:59.534 --> 00:14:01.000
-Oops.
-He's like, "Oh."

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:03.367
He's like, "Okay.
Well, where do I go?"

00:14:03.367 --> 00:14:05.901
So we had to pass
the presidential motorcade,

00:14:05.901 --> 00:14:10.501
then the VP, and then we went
way back, and then it was ours.

00:14:10.501 --> 00:14:13.100
Because you go
in descending order.

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:14.734
-You're like,
"But I was just..."

00:14:14.734 --> 00:14:16.934
-But the thing was,
we were so glad

00:14:16.934 --> 00:14:21.100
to get in that back motorcade
and to go home...

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:22.801
-Yeah.
-...to our house.

00:14:22.801 --> 00:14:24.167
-To your actual house.

00:14:24.167 --> 00:14:26.334
-You know, that's
the progress of democracy.

00:14:26.334 --> 00:14:28.467
You do your time,
you pass it on,

00:14:28.467 --> 00:14:31.300
you let the next president lead.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:35.300
And so it was --
it's kind of a relief.

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:37.567
-In this book,

00:14:37.567 --> 00:14:40.067
there's so many great stories
about your life,

00:14:40.067 --> 00:14:42.801
but there's also tips
on how to deal with

00:14:42.801 --> 00:14:45.400
what we're dealing with,
with everything,

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:48.667
with what we all --
isolation with COVID.

00:14:48.667 --> 00:14:51.868
And just everything is kind of
touched in this book.

00:14:51.868 --> 00:14:55.267
And one thing I loved
is "the power of small."

00:14:55.267 --> 00:14:58.667
-Yeah, yeah, that's one of
the early chapters because --

00:14:58.667 --> 00:15:02.868
I wrote this chapter
particularly for young people

00:15:02.868 --> 00:15:06.133
who are feeling so anxious
about the state of the world.

00:15:06.133 --> 00:15:07.734
I mean, no wonder.

00:15:07.734 --> 00:15:10.434
Climate change,
riots on Capitol Hill,

00:15:10.434 --> 00:15:12.467
gun violence, on and on.

00:15:12.467 --> 00:15:16.000
And these young kids,
young kids as young as 15,

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:18.434
are trying to figure out
how to solve these problems,

00:15:18.434 --> 00:15:20.100
and they're getting all twisted

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:22.901
because they don't yet
have the power

00:15:22.901 --> 00:15:25.334
because they're trying to
bite off too much, too soon.

00:15:25.334 --> 00:15:27.701
And so "the power of small"
is to focus

00:15:27.701 --> 00:15:29.934
on what you can
uniquely control.

00:15:29.934 --> 00:15:31.767
And for a kid in high school,

00:15:31.767 --> 00:15:34.501
it's graduating
from high school.

00:15:34.501 --> 00:15:37.701
You know, get your education
because you can't do that --

00:15:37.701 --> 00:15:39.868
You can't end violence
in your neighborhood

00:15:39.868 --> 00:15:42.834
unless you focus
on what's at hand.

00:15:42.834 --> 00:15:45.234
So starting small,
even for us as adults,

00:15:45.234 --> 00:15:47.534
is about focus
on your own knitting,

00:15:47.534 --> 00:15:49.400
because there's power in that.

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:51.968
You know, if each of us
just raise the children

00:15:51.968 --> 00:15:53.567
we bring into the world --

00:15:53.567 --> 00:15:56.501
you know, if we did nothing else
but do that --

00:15:56.501 --> 00:15:59.834
oh, just imagine
where we would be as a nation,

00:15:59.834 --> 00:16:02.734
as a human race,
if we just took care of that,

00:16:02.734 --> 00:16:05.000
if we showed them
the love and the focus

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.200
and if we put our time
and energy into that

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:10.067
instead of trying to solve
all the big problems.

00:16:10.067 --> 00:16:12.000
So change happens
in small bites.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:13.467
It's not just the big stuff.

00:16:13.467 --> 00:16:14.868
-Yeah, because it starts small,

00:16:14.868 --> 00:16:16.567
then it goes to a family,
then it goes to a community.

00:16:16.567 --> 00:16:19.601
And it was great.
There's also a chapter

00:16:19.601 --> 00:16:22.968
dedicated to
"when they go low, we go high."

00:16:22.968 --> 00:16:26.601
-Well, people
still keep asking me,

00:16:26.601 --> 00:16:29.801
"You still mean that?"
-Yeah.

00:16:29.801 --> 00:16:33.100
But that was --
that was an epic line.

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:37.267
That was just --
It stuck in everyone's head.

00:16:37.267 --> 00:16:40.400
Did you realize how powerful
that would be years after?

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:44.300
-No, no. I didn't know
I'd be known for that line.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:46.400
But I'm proud of that.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:49.701
People ask me,
do I still -- do I ever go low?

00:16:49.701 --> 00:16:52.400
And, yeah, I go low.

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:53.934
-You do.
-I go low a lot of times.

00:16:53.934 --> 00:16:56.901
But I think for people
who have a platform,

00:16:56.901 --> 00:17:00.100
who are being looked up to,

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:02.801
we have a responsibility
to stay high

00:17:02.801 --> 00:17:05.300
because kids are watching us.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.901
If you need to go low,
go low at home.

00:17:07.901 --> 00:17:10.334
Do it with your kitchen table,
with your spouse.

00:17:10.334 --> 00:17:15.000
But we set the example,
because leaders set the tone.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:19.534
And we felt what it's felt like
to be led with low,

00:17:19.534 --> 00:17:21.634
and it didn't feel great.

00:17:21.634 --> 00:17:24.834
That's why it's important.
It's a practice. It's a habit.

00:17:24.834 --> 00:17:27.300
It's something we show
the next generation,

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:29.734
that we don't --
That doesn't mean we settle.

00:17:29.734 --> 00:17:31.467
That doesn't mean
we're complacent.

00:17:31.467 --> 00:17:33.501
It means we think
before we speak,

00:17:33.501 --> 00:17:36.234
because words matter,
and I still believe that.

00:17:36.234 --> 00:17:39.234
And I try the best that I can
when I'm in the public eye,

00:17:39.234 --> 00:17:41.434
when I know that kids
are watching,

00:17:41.434 --> 00:17:44.167
to stay high
because high begets high.

00:17:44.167 --> 00:17:45.634
-That's right.

00:17:45.634 --> 00:17:47.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.567
Now, uh...

00:17:52.567 --> 00:17:54.634
you went on a book tour
around the country,

00:17:54.634 --> 00:17:56.434
and you had
amazing conversations

00:17:56.434 --> 00:17:58.567
with amazing people.

00:17:58.567 --> 00:18:01.434
Our pal Gayle King, who we love.
Tyler Perry.

00:18:01.434 --> 00:18:02.901
-Lots of great people.
-Hoda Kotb.

00:18:02.901 --> 00:18:04.934
-David Letterman.
-David Letterman.

00:18:04.934 --> 00:18:08.300
-Oh, so many.
Sure. Conan O'Brien. Sure.

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:10.033
-Alright. Alright.
You were busy.

00:18:10.033 --> 00:18:12.701
-Yeah, yeah. It's interesting.

00:18:12.701 --> 00:18:14.334
But, you know,
there's always time

00:18:14.334 --> 00:18:17.467
for another book, you know?
Um...

00:18:17.467 --> 00:18:19.267
But the list is --
Amazing people.

00:18:19.267 --> 00:18:21.067
And you turned it
into a podcast.

00:18:21.067 --> 00:18:22.300
-Yeah.
-And this is great.

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:24.200
It's a great thing.
You have to listen to it.

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:26.400
It's such great conversation.
-"The Light Podcast."

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:28.167
So, for those
who missed the book tour,

00:18:28.167 --> 00:18:29.968
we've recorded
every conversation,

00:18:29.968 --> 00:18:31.868
edited it down
to the best of the best.

00:18:31.868 --> 00:18:33.567
And, you know,
it touches on the book,

00:18:33.567 --> 00:18:36.968
but we also each share
our own stories of finding light

00:18:36.968 --> 00:18:39.200
and developing friendships
and relationships.

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:41.601
It's really a fun
set of conversations

00:18:41.601 --> 00:18:43.467
that are now on Audible.

00:18:43.467 --> 00:18:46.467
-And then you have --
One of the interviews

00:18:46.467 --> 00:18:49.167
or the conversations
are with Oprah Winfrey.

00:18:49.167 --> 00:18:51.934
-The one and only.
-Yeah. Oprah Winfrey.

00:18:51.934 --> 00:18:53.534
And it airs --
-That Oprah.

00:18:53.534 --> 00:18:55.300
-That Oprah.

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:57.968
And it's airing on April 25th
on Netflix as a special.

00:18:57.968 --> 00:19:01.300
-Yeah, that will be televised,
because when you're with Oprah,

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:03.100
you just televise it.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:04.801
-I just feel like, yeah,
you have to televise it.

00:19:04.801 --> 00:19:06.634
Yeah, but you have
actually become friends

00:19:06.634 --> 00:19:08.934
with Oprah Winfrey.
You guys are really --

00:19:08.934 --> 00:19:12.033
-I mean, Oprah was one of
the very early supporters

00:19:12.033 --> 00:19:14.567
before Barack
was even thinking about running.

00:19:14.567 --> 00:19:17.367
I mean, she spotted him
when he was a U.S. senator.

00:19:17.367 --> 00:19:19.834
And she has sort of been --

00:19:19.834 --> 00:19:23.334
Oprah is that person
who never asks for anything

00:19:23.334 --> 00:19:27.567
but is always there with her
generosity and love and support.

00:19:27.567 --> 00:19:30.567
There isn't anything
you can't ask her to do

00:19:30.567 --> 00:19:33.701
if you're a friend
that she won't try to do.

00:19:33.701 --> 00:19:36.801
She is who she appears to be.
-Yeah.

00:19:36.801 --> 00:19:38.767
-And I am lucky
to call her a friend.

00:19:38.767 --> 00:19:40.934
-I want to show a clip
from your special.

00:19:40.934 --> 00:19:42.734
Here's a look
at "The Light We Carry:

00:19:42.734 --> 00:19:45.267
Michelle Obama
and Oprah Winfrey."

00:19:45.267 --> 00:19:47.133
-When you first arrived
in Hawaii,

00:19:47.133 --> 00:19:49.400
you were looking for --
and I understand why --

00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:51.234
You were a working woman
and had never been to Hawaii.

00:19:51.234 --> 00:19:53.968
So you're looking for the Hawaii
in "Hawaii Five-0."

00:19:53.968 --> 00:19:55.734
Those of you who remember
that show.

00:19:55.734 --> 00:19:58.667
-Mai Tais
and sunsets on the beach.

00:19:58.667 --> 00:20:01.567
-And honeymoon suites.
Instead...

00:20:01.567 --> 00:20:04.701
-Yeah, instead, it was a trip
home to visit his family.

00:20:04.701 --> 00:20:06.267
That's where he was from.

00:20:06.267 --> 00:20:08.767
He wasn't going back
to some island vacation.

00:20:08.767 --> 00:20:11.334
He was going back
to be with his people.

00:20:11.334 --> 00:20:15.067
But I was young and I was --
It was cold in Chicago,

00:20:15.067 --> 00:20:17.133
and I thought, "I'm going
to Hawaii with my man.

00:20:17.133 --> 00:20:18.667
It's going to be so romantic."

00:20:18.667 --> 00:20:21.167
[ Laughter ]

00:20:21.167 --> 00:20:23.300
And then we landed,
and we went straight

00:20:23.300 --> 00:20:26.033
to Toot and Gramps' apartment.
No ocean.

00:20:26.033 --> 00:20:28.133
It was a high-rise building.

00:20:28.133 --> 00:20:30.501
Go up to the 10th floor.
You know, walk in.

00:20:30.501 --> 00:20:32.934
Looks like
my grandparents' house.

00:20:32.934 --> 00:20:35.367
Might as well be on
the South Side of Chicago.

00:20:35.367 --> 00:20:39.067
-[ Laughs ]
Isn't that reality? I love it.

00:20:39.067 --> 00:20:41.133
Michelle Obama, everybody.

00:20:41.133 --> 00:20:42.434
"The Light We Carry:

00:20:42.434 --> 00:20:44.267
Michelle Obama
and Oprah Winfrey"

00:20:44.267 --> 00:20:47.267
premieres April 25th on Netflix.

00:20:47.267 --> 00:20:48.601
More "Tonight Show"
after the break.

00:20:48.601 --> 00:20:50.467
Stick around.
Thank you, as always.

